{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the population mean $\\mu =35,$ we have that $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=35.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-16b6352b-b794-5e9b-8e89-998162234721"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-16b6352b-b794-5e9b-8e89-998162234721" :answer-field/id #uuid "16b6352b-b794-5e9b-8e89-998162234721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-de90b455-9769-5002-b420-cf8f26b995dc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-73ef06c8-8da1-5159-899f-a783f0d9b533" "answer-de90b455-9769-5002-b420-cf8f26b995dc" "answer-521dbf69-204b-5e22-8554-c486c78da458" "answer-db626626-d704-5af6-990d-0f4a8993817f" "answer-02643f9d-582b-581f-994d-46c4ac76a48c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73ef06c8-8da1-5159-899f-a783f0d9b533" :answer/id #uuid "73ef06c8-8da1-5159-899f-a783f0d9b533" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de90b455-9769-5002-b420-cf8f26b995dc" :answer/id #uuid "de90b455-9769-5002-b420-cf8f26b995dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-521dbf69-204b-5e22-8554-c486c78da458" :answer/id #uuid "521dbf69-204b-5e22-8554-c486c78da458" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16}{35}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db626626-d704-5af6-990d-0f4a8993817f" :answer/id #uuid "db626626-d704-5af6-990d-0f4a8993817f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{35}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02643f9d-582b-581f-994d-46c4ac76a48c" :answer/id #uuid "02643f9d-582b-581f-994d-46c4ac76a48c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{35}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ebb176a9-8df7-5364-a45a-43a1450a346c" :question/id #uuid "ebb176a9-8df7-5364-a45a-43a1450a346c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143251" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ is a sample of size $n=30$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =10$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=4,$ what is $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the population mean $\\mu =10,$ we have that $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=10.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dff61a48-69b5-5fd7-952c-7afcd7d49156"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dff61a48-69b5-5fd7-952c-7afcd7d49156" :answer-field/id #uuid "dff61a48-69b5-5fd7-952c-7afcd7d49156" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b58cfd9e-a4a8-5cf3-b52d-9bbdad20be84" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b58cfd9e-a4a8-5cf3-b52d-9bbdad20be84" "answer-773ce66b-3041-5254-bb07-1e42e838402d" "answer-13e0a3d0-6ab2-5b3b-be81-f9ce0222fb6e" "answer-14e8de67-0e76-51ab-94c6-b148785759f5" "answer-9ac4604a-d85a-5095-974d-d023522a4890"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b58cfd9e-a4a8-5cf3-b52d-9bbdad20be84" :answer/id #uuid "b58cfd9e-a4a8-5cf3-b52d-9bbdad20be84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-773ce66b-3041-5254-bb07-1e42e838402d" :answer/id #uuid "773ce66b-3041-5254-bb07-1e42e838402d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13e0a3d0-6ab2-5b3b-be81-f9ce0222fb6e" :answer/id #uuid "13e0a3d0-6ab2-5b3b-be81-f9ce0222fb6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14e8de67-0e76-51ab-94c6-b148785759f5" :answer/id #uuid "14e8de67-0e76-51ab-94c6-b148785759f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ac4604a-d85a-5095-974d-d023522a4890" :answer/id #uuid "9ac4604a-d85a-5095-974d-d023522a4890" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb20853b-4548-57d9-b738-08eff2a48018" :question/id #uuid "bb20853b-4548-57d9-b738-08eff2a48018" :question/math-academy-id "q-143287" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2}$ of two bolts is drawn from a population. The population has bolts of two different lengths, $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{8}\\,\\text{cm},$ distributed in the ratio $2:1.$ Calculate $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}].$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nWe're told that the bolts in the population have lengths $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{8}\\,\\text{cm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $2:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean, we can consider a population consisting of $2+1=3$ bolts only with the following lengths:\n\n$$\n5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,8\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot 5+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(10+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{3}. \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu =6\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-379b4b8b-013a-5a9b-8bd8-ddcccbd4e2ef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-379b4b8b-013a-5a9b-8bd8-ddcccbd4e2ef" :answer-field/id #uuid "379b4b8b-013a-5a9b-8bd8-ddcccbd4e2ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ec3e6ae7-41da-5e42-94d1-edee1b8cf018" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7a9be8b9-fbb8-5034-a55d-f530a53a0969" "answer-7711b3da-ec81-51bb-a8b1-cc66352671e8" "answer-e2541e14-e5d3-5bb3-9765-5c6a45e991aa" "answer-ec3e6ae7-41da-5e42-94d1-edee1b8cf018" "answer-cfc00a78-c8ed-5640-8fa8-0a86f580e4cb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a9be8b9-fbb8-5034-a55d-f530a53a0969" :answer/id #uuid "7a9be8b9-fbb8-5034-a55d-f530a53a0969" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7711b3da-ec81-51bb-a8b1-cc66352671e8" :answer/id #uuid "7711b3da-ec81-51bb-a8b1-cc66352671e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.25\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2541e14-e5d3-5bb3-9765-5c6a45e991aa" :answer/id #uuid "e2541e14-e5d3-5bb3-9765-5c6a45e991aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec3e6ae7-41da-5e42-94d1-edee1b8cf018" :answer/id #uuid "ec3e6ae7-41da-5e42-94d1-edee1b8cf018" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfc00a78-c8ed-5640-8fa8-0a86f580e4cb" :answer/id #uuid "cfc00a78-c8ed-5640-8fa8-0a86f580e4cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-da2180f8-2611-5385-974d-058bcc233c80" :question/id #uuid "da2180f8-2611-5385-974d-058bcc233c80" :question/math-academy-id "q-143250" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{80}$ is a sample of size $n=80$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =15.5$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=8,$ what is $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the population mean $\\mu =15.5,$ we have that $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=15.5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6ef3bddc-ef5d-50ca-8a4d-2b1353c3d119"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6ef3bddc-ef5d-50ca-8a4d-2b1353c3d119" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ef3bddc-ef5d-50ca-8a4d-2b1353c3d119" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2eb48f9f-c8ee-5cda-9136-b3a735e840ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2939ee74-3320-5827-9605-fa3dc1556af8" "answer-2eb48f9f-c8ee-5cda-9136-b3a735e840ea" "answer-7ae7c34e-7399-5c2f-b0ad-1c8eda0758de" "answer-00d00815-bcdc-5d4c-8d9c-9110e655684d" "answer-38449440-ecca-5bf1-a518-8190e6795ae1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2939ee74-3320-5827-9605-fa3dc1556af8" :answer/id #uuid "2939ee74-3320-5827-9605-fa3dc1556af8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "124" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2eb48f9f-c8ee-5cda-9136-b3a735e840ea" :answer/id #uuid "2eb48f9f-c8ee-5cda-9136-b3a735e840ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ae7c34e-7399-5c2f-b0ad-1c8eda0758de" :answer/id #uuid "7ae7c34e-7399-5c2f-b0ad-1c8eda0758de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00d00815-bcdc-5d4c-8d9c-9110e655684d" :answer/id #uuid "00d00815-bcdc-5d4c-8d9c-9110e655684d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15.5}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38449440-ecca-5bf1-a518-8190e6795ae1" :answer/id #uuid "38449440-ecca-5bf1-a518-8190e6795ae1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15.5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158531 :tutorial/content "In statistics, we often want to use sample data to deduce (or **infer**) the values of various **population parameters**. The two most common population parameters that we wish to find are\n\n- the **population mean** $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the **population variance** $\\sigma^2.$\n\nThe population mean is the mean of the *entire* population. Likewise, the population variance is the variance of the *entire* population. We should consider these values as *fixed* unknowns. \n\nSuppose, for example, we want to find the mean number of pages per book at a particular library. In other words, we wish to find the population mean $\\mu.$ However, it is impractical to count the pages in every book. So, instead, we might approximate $\\mu$ using the following sampling procedure:\n\n1\\. Randomly choose, say, $5$ books off the shelf.\n\n2\\. Count the number of pages in each book.\n\n3\\. Compute the mean of the $5$ observations.\n\nIn this case, the **sample size** $n=5.$\n\nSuppose we follow this procedure and get the following **observations**:\n\n$$\n201, \\qquad 506, \\qquad 58, \\qquad 104, \\qquad 99\n$$\n\nIt's convenient to denote the individual observations as $x_i.$ So, in this case, we have \n\n$$\nx_1 = 201,\\qquad x_2 = 506,\\qquad \\ldots,\\qquad x_5 = 99.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the mean $\\overline{x}$ of our sample as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(201+506+58+104+99) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 968 \\\\  & =193.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The value $\\bar{x} = 193.6$ is called an **estimate** of the population mean $\\mu.$ We sometimes write $\\hat{\\mu} = 193.6$ to denote that the value $193.6$ is an estimate of $\\mu.$\n\nIt's important to realize that our estimate $\\bar{x}$ depends entirely on this particular sample. We'd get a different estimate for $\\mu$ if we took a different sample." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]}]
